## AI Agent to chat with Airtable and analyze data
**Made by [Mark Shcherbakov]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }} from community [5minAI]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

Engaging with data stored in Airtable often requires manual navigation and time-consuming searches. This workflow allows users to interact conversationally with their datasets, retrieving essential information quickly while minimizing the need for complex queries.

This workflow enables an AI agent to facilitate

## 1. Retrieve Meeting Transcript
[Read more about working with HTTP node]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

There's no built-in support for Google Meets transcript API however, we can solve this problem with the HTTP node. Note you may also need to setup a separate Google OAuth API Credential to obtain the required scopes.

## 2. Let AI Agent Carry Out Follow-Up Actions
[Read more about working with AI Agents]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

The big difference between Basic LLM chains and AI Agents is that AI agents are given the automony to perform actions. Provided the right tool exists, AI Agents can send emails, book flights and even order pizza! Here we're leaving it up to our agent to book any follow-up meetings after the call and invite all interested parties.

## Step 1. Import Survey Responses
[Read more about Google Sheets]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

Our approach requires to import all participant responses as vectors with metadata linking them to the questions being answered. To do this, we'll generate questiona and answer pairs from the survey.

## Step 2. Vectorize Each Response Into Qdrant
[Read more about using Qdrant]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

Special attention is given to how metadata is captured as it becomes key to this workflow is being able to retrieve subsets of the data for analysis.

## Step 3. Trigger Insights SubWorkflow
[Learn more about Workflow Triggers]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

A subworkflow is used to trigger the analysis for the survey. This separation is optional but used here to better demonstrate the two part process.

## Step 4. Create Insights Sheet
[Learn more about Workflow Triggers]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

To capture the generated insights, we'll create a new unique sheet within the survey spreadsheet. This is optional and you may want to capture in other datastores depending on your needs.

## Step 5. Get List Of Questions From Survey
[Read more about using Google Sheets]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

Next we'll fetch the survey for metadata and questions, splitting them into separate workflow items. Our intention is to process each question end-to-end before moving to the next. This approach is a little "safer" in the scenario where an interruption occurs we won't lose all our work.

## 2. Sentiment Analysis on Current Issue Activity
[Learn more about the Information Extractor node]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

With our recently updated posts, we can use our AI to perform a quick sentiment analysis on the ongoing conversation to check the overall mood of the support issue. This is a great way to check how things are generally going in the support queue; positive should be normal but negative could indicate some uncomfortableness or even frustration.

## 3. Using AI to Analyse Article Structure and Writing Styles
[Read more about the Basic LLM Chain node]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

Our approach is to first perform a high-level analysis of all available articles in order to replicate their content layout and writing styles. This will act as a guideline to help the AI to structure our future articles.

## 4. Using AI to Extract Voice Characteristics and Traits
[Read more about the Information Extractor node]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

Second, we'll use AI to analysis the brand voice characteristics of the previous articles. This picks out the tone, style and choice of language used and identifies them into categories. These categories will be used as guidelines for the AI to keep the future article consistent in tone and voice.

## 6. Save Draft to Wordpress
[Learn more about the Wordpress node]({{ $env.WEBHOOK_URL }}

To close out the template, we'll simple save our generated article as a draft which could allow human team members to review and validate the article before publishing.
